	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the tap</h2>
	<p>
		The CB-to-tablet cable is RJ45 carrying RS-485 <em>and</em> ~14 V power. Identify pins
		by number, not colour (T568A vs T568B swap the pairs):
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>pin</th><th>signal</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>1</td><td>RS-485 B(+)</td></tr>
			<tr><td>2</td><td>RS-485 A(−)</td></tr>
			<tr><td>4, 6</td><td>GND</td></tr>
			<tr><td>5</td><td>~14 V DC (measure before connecting!)</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<Mermaid>{`flowchart LR
		subgraph CB["Control Box RJ45"]
			P12["pins 1–2<br/>RS-485 B+/A−"]
			P46["pins 4/6 — GND"]
			P5["pin 5 — ~14 V DC"]
		end
		P12 --> TS["12-way terminal strip"]
		P46 --> TS
		P5 --> FUSE["1 A T fuse"] --> BUCK["buck → 5 V micro-USB"] --> PWR["Pi Zero W PWR"]
		TS -->|"A/B/GND"| RS["USB-RS485 dongle"] --> OTG["90° left OTG"] --> USBP["Pi Zero W USB (OTG)"]
		TS -->|"GND"| BUCK
		TS -->|"~14 V + GND<br/>(power only)"| JOIN["ethernet joiner"] --> OEM["OEM cable"] --> TABP["Wall tablet<br/>(power only; data pair disconnected)"]`}</Mermaid>

	<p>
		The final wrinkle: the wall tablet still needs power, and its OEM cable used to get
		both power <em>and</em> data from the CB. The finished build forks a second
		power-only feed off the terminal block (~14 V + GND, no A/B pair) through an ethernet
		joiner into the OEM cable. The tablet keeps its always-on wall power, its FTDI data
		path to the CB is physically disconnected, and all control flows over Wi-Fi to the Pi's
		WebSocket API. Same topology will serve the eventual Home Assistant setup — the wall
		tablet becomes a dumb-ish panel regardless of what software is asking the daemon
		questions.
	</p>

	<div class="callout warn">
		<span class="callout-label">rules</span>
		Fuse goes on the ~14 V line <em>before</em> the buck. Common ground everywhere. And the
		cardinal rule: never two tablet-side talkers on the same RS-485 segment — with the
		power-only fork, the tablet's data pair isn't even connected, so the rule enforces
		itself.
	</div>

