	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the software appliance</h2>
	<p>
		The Pi runs <strong>Alpine Linux</strong> (32-bit ARMv6, OpenRC) — small, musl-based,
		and happy on the Zero W. The daemon is cross-compiled as a static musl binary with <a
			href="https://github.com/cross-rs/cross">cross</a
		>:
	</p>
	<pre><code>cargo install cross --git https://github.com/cross-rs/cross
./scripts/build-pi-zero.sh   # → arm-unknown-linux-musleabihf/release/cb-daemon
scp target/arm-unknown-linux-musleabihf/release/cb-daemon \
    root@&lt;pi&gt;:/usr/local/bin/cb-daemon</code></pre>

	<p>Config (<code>/etc/cb-daemon/config.toml</code>):</p>
	<pre><code>backend   = "tty"
device    = "/dev/ttyUSB0"
bind      = "0.0.0.0:2026"
tty_baud  = 57600
unit_id_hint = "xxxxx"     # your CB's unit id
log_level = "debug"</code></pre>

	<p>
		OpenRC runs it under <code>supervise-daemon</code> (unlimited respawn, waits for <code>/dev/ttyUSB0</code> at boot). One Alpine gotcha worth publishing: OpenRC 0.63's
		supervise-daemon captures <em>no</em> child output unless you set <code
			>output_log</code
		>/<code>error_log</code> — without them, logs vanish silently.
	</p>

	<h3>RAM-only logging, and the ENOSPC deadlock</h3>
	<p>
		<code>/var/log</code> is a 128 MiB tmpfs — the SD card does no log writes. Logrotate
		caps usage with <code>copytruncate</code>… and this produced the best failure of the
		project. At debug level the daemon can emit ~70 KB/s of frame logs; with logrotate on
		a 15-minute timer, the live log could outgrow the <em>free space remaining</em> between
		runs. <code>copytruncate</code> then needs to copy a file that no longer fits, every
		rotation fails ENOSPC, and since nothing can ever be freed, the tmpfs stays 100% full
		forever — a permanent deadlock that also kills the daemon's logs. The fix: run
		logrotate every minute (<code>/etc/periodic/1min</code>), bounding worst-case growth to
		~9 MB, and keep exactly one periodic copy (two timers colliding on the state lock exit
		with ALERTs). Boring problem, satisfying fix.
	</p>
	<p>
		Also: <code>gpu_mem=32</code> in <code>/boot/config.txt</code> reclaims ~32 MB on a
		headless node (re-apply after bootloader upgrades — Alpine preserves it for normal
		updates but not those).
	</p>

