	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>cutover</h2>
	<p>
		Bus verification first — with nothing talking, the CB's endless <code>Ping</code> heartbeat
		confirms wiring, then a one-shot probe confirms negotiation:	</p>
	<pre><code>stty -F /dev/ttyUSB0 57600 raw -echo
cat /dev/ttyUSB0                        # &lt;U&gt;Ping&lt;/U=db&gt; &lt;U&gt;Ping&lt;/U=db&gt; …
echo -n "&lt;U&gt;getSystemData&lt;/U=15&gt;" &gt; /dev/ttyUSB0
# → &lt;U&gt;CAN2 in use&lt;/U=95&gt;</code></pre>

	<p>Then the handover — tablet daemon off, tablet service repointed at the Pi:</p>
	<pre><code># on the tablet: stop the local daemon, disable its Magisk module
su -c '/data/adb/cb-daemon/control.sh stop'
su -c 'touch /data/adb/modules/cb-daemon/disable'

# repoint aaservice2 at the Pi
su -c 'am start-foreground-service \
  -n com.air.advantage.aaservice2/.service.UartForegroundService \
  -a com.air.advantage.TRANSPORT_MODE_CHANGED \
  --es daemon_ws_url "ws://&lt;pi&gt;:2026/v1/mailbox-stream" \
  --es transport_mode ws'</code></pre>

	<p>
		One app-side gotcha: Android's network security policy blocks cleartext <code
			>ws://</code
		> to non-loopback hosts, so the rebuilt aaservice ships a <code
			>network_security_config</code
		> with <code>cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"</code> (fine here — the link is an
		isolated IoT VLAN, and the protocol has no secrets worth encrypting).
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>verification</h2>
	<pre><code># on the Pi
rc-service cb-daemon status        # started
tail /var/log/cb-daemon.log        # "negotiated detail=CAN2 in use"
netstat -tn | grep 2026            # ESTABLISHED from the tablet

# on the tablet — wall panel works end-to-end through the Pi,
# and the vendor :2025 HTTP API (home automation) never noticed</code></pre>

	<p>
		The stack has already survived its hardest live test: re-pairing a wireless temperature
		sensor. That's the most convoluted flow in the whole system — RF pairing chatter on the
		bus, register 12 events through the daemon, out the WebSocket, through aaservice2's
		broadcast mapping, into MyPlace's pairing UI, and the attach write all the way back —
		and it completed perfectly. If that works, everything works.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>where it stands</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>The Pi is the sole talker on the CB bus; the tablet is a pure WebSocket client, powered via the power-only fork</li>
		<li>The vendor UI is 100% functional — zones, temps, modes, schedules, sensor pairing</li>
		<li>
			Complete rollback: unplug the fork, plug the OEM cable back into the CB, and flip
			aaservice2 back to USB mode — the same configuration as the OEM software stack,
			just running our lobotomised MyPlace and our aaservice rewrite
		</li>
		<li>
			The Pi Zero W was simply what was on hand — any SBC with Linux and a USB port can
			fill this role. An MCU (ESP32, Pico W) could too, but that path needs custom Rust
			firmware that doesn't exist yet
		</li>
		<li>
			The endgame: retire the vendor apps entirely — a Home Assistant integration against
			the daemon's broker API, with HA running on the wall tablet itself, powered by the
			same power-only fork
		</li>
	</ul>

